components.analogy:
Before CSS Grid, frontend engineers built complex pages with <code>float</code>, <code>position: absolute</code>, or even <code>table</code> layouts — fragile code that was hard to maintain. Even once Flexbox arrived and greatly improved one-dimensional (row or column) layout, real product pages often need to control <b>both rows and columns at once</b>:
/* Goal: a fixed sidebar + a two-column content area + a footer spanning the full width */
/* The Flexbox approach: nest several layers of flex containers, logic scattered and hard to maintain */
CSS Grid is purpose-built for <b>two-dimensional layout</b>, letting developers define both row and column structure in a single declaration, so the intent of a complex layout is obvious right in the code.

components.example:
.dashboard {
  display: grid;
  /* Keep the sidebar at 240px; the three 1fr tracks divide the remaining space equally so the sidebar does not distort content proportions. */
  grid-template-columns: 240px 1fr 1fr 1fr;
  /* Fix the top navigation at 80px, let the middle absorb remaining space, and keep the status bar at 60px. */
  grid-template-rows: 80px auto 60px;
  gap: 16px;  /* Use one grid gap instead of scattered margins, which keeps the layout easier to maintain. */
}

/* The footer spans all 4 columns; -1 means the final grid line, so the column count need not be repeated. */
.footer {
  grid-column: 1 / -1;
}

/* Approach 2: named areas are more readable because the layout diagram appears directly in CSS. */
.layout {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 200px 1fr;
  grid-template-rows: 60px auto 40px;
  grid-template-areas:
    "header  header"
    "sidebar main  "
    "footer  footer";
}
.header  { grid-area: header; }  /* The header spans both columns. */
.sidebar { grid-area: sidebar; } /* The fixed-width navigation area on the left. */
.main    { grid-area: main; }    /* The flexible primary content area on the right. */
.footer  { grid-area: footer; }  /* The footer spans both columns again. */

components.content:
With traditional media queries, you end up writing a pile of `@media (max-width: 768px)` breakpoints for phones, tablets, and desktops. CSS Grid offers a responsive card-grid formula that needs zero media queries:

```css
/* The zero-media-query responsive card grid magic formula */
.card-grid {
  display: grid;
  /* auto-fit fills available space + minmax sets column width bounds */
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr));
  gap: 20px;
}
```

> 💡 **Breaking down the formula**:
> 1. **`minmax(280px, 1fr)`**: each card column is never narrower than `280px`, and stretches evenly (`1fr`) when there's extra space;
> 2. **`auto-fit`**: when the screen gets too narrow to fit another 280px column, empty columns automatically collapse, pushing the cards below to wrap;
> 3. **The result**: from a 320px iPhone to a 4K display, the card grid responds and wraps seamlessly — with not a single line of `@media`!

questions.question:
In the following CSS Grid code, what does <code>.item { grid-column: 1 / -1; }</code> do?
.container {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
}
.item {
  grid-column: 1 / -1;
}
